<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John George I, Elector of Saxony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1585">1585</a>–<a href="/years/1656">1656</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John George I was Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> from 1611 to 1656. A Lutheran loyal to the emperor, he helped crush the Bohemian revolt and gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>, but later joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> before making the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1635">Peace of Prague</a> in 1635. His will created three secondary duchies for his younger sons: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-weissenfels">Saxe-Weissenfels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-merseburg">Saxe-Merseburg</a> and Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-george-i-elector-of-saxony.jpg" alt="John George I, Elector of Saxony" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frans Luycx · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Georg_I_Saxony.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John George was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> on 5 March 1585, the second son of Elector Christian I and Sophie of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, and belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-line">Albertine line</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a>. To learn statecraft, law and history he and his brothers matriculated at Leipzig University, with which he kept close ties throughout his reign, often employing its medical and legal professors as advisors and physicians. He succeeded his elder brother Christian II as elector on 23 June 1611.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>'s geographical position gave its ruler weight in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> more than its standing among German Protestants did. John George was a sound Lutheran, but he disliked the growing strength of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> and the prestige of the Palatinate, and he favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. In August 1619 he voted for Archduke Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> as emperor, and he occupied Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> for him, so taking part in driving Frederick V, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. Unease at imperial policy grew, and the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">Restitution</a> of March 1629 deepened his fears, yet he only protested, calling a meeting of Protestant princes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in February 1631.</p><p>Tilly's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> drove him into an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> in September 1631. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> were routed at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-breitenfeld-1631">Breitenfeld</a>, and the elector fled. They later occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, but Wallenstein drove them back. In May 1635 he made the Treaty of Prague with Ferdinand II, receiving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a> and other territory, and then declared war on the Swedes but was beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittstock">Wittstock</a> in October 1636. Saxony, ravaged by both sides, agreed a truce with the Swedes in September 1645, which ended the war there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>John George died on 8 October 1656, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a>, which for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> did little more than confirm the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. John George II succeeded him, and three younger sons, Augustus, Christian and Maurice, inherited the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weissenfels</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a>. Swedish soldiers called him Öl Göran, Beer Göran, for his drinking and eating. An assessment holds that, though not without political skill, he was not a great ruler: his character was harsh, and Wallenstein spoke of him with contempt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John George I of Saxony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Thirty Years’ War <b>John George I</b> first stayed neutral, then joined Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden; the Saxon contingent fled at the Battle of Breitenfeld in 1631, and Saxony suffered heavily from both sides before its separate Peace of Prague with the emperor in 1635.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Elector John George</b> I of Saxony divided parts of his lands in his will among his younger sons.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-merseburg">Duchy of Saxe-Merseburg</a></li><li><blockquote>After Elector <b>John George I</b> of Saxony died in 1656, his son Augustus, already administrator of the Archbishopric of Magdeburg, received Weissenfels, Querfurt and other lands under his father's will, founding the Duchy of Saxe-Weissenfels.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weissenfels">Duchy of Saxe-Weissenfels</a></li><li><blockquote>In his will, <b>Elector John George</b> I of Saxony created three secondary duchies for his younger sons.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-zeitz">Duchy of Saxe-Zeitz</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.05,13.74&amp;year=1631" aria-label="Dresden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4862 394 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John George I, Elector of Saxony"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5312 664h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.05,13.74&amp;year=1631">What was here in 1631? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
